<CounterfeitStudy
  title="The Ascetic and the Prophet"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Twenty-Ninth of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="The robe or the word: which marks the man of God?"
  description="The twenty-ninth of the 38 Blessings, seeing the monk, set beside Scripture's test of a man of God: not his robe but Yahuah's words in his mouth."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>The twenty-ninth blessing, "Recognizing the ascetic," comes from the Pali <em>samaṇānañca dassanaṃ</em>, "seeing recluses." In Thailand the monks walk out at dawn in saffron robes, carrying their bowls, and laypeople kneel by the road to give them food. To see a monk, hear him, and serve him is counted a blessing. Buddhist teaching calls the monk a "field of merit" (<em>puññakkhetta</em>): what is planted in him by a gift comes back multiplied to the giver.</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Israel had its own holy men. Saul's servant told him of "a man of God" in the city, "an honourable man; all that he saith cometh surely to pass," and they went to the seer for counsel (1 Samuel 9:6-9). Elijah was known by "an hairy garment" and "a girdle of leather" (2 Kings 1:8), and John the Immerser dressed the same way (Matthew 3:4). Torah even provided the Nazarite vow, by which a man could "separate himself unto Yahuah" for a set time (Numbers 6:2).</p>
    <p>So the picture of a set-apart man, plainly dressed and sought out for counsel, has a real root in Scripture.</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>The mark of a prophet in Scripture was never his clothing. It was Yahuah's word: "I will put my words in his mouth; and he shall speak unto them all that I shall command him" (Deuteronomy 18:18). The test was whether his word came to pass (Deuteronomy 18:22) and whether it led the people to Yahuah and His commandments or away from them (Deuteronomy 13:1-4).</p>
    <p>Dress proved nothing. Zechariah foretold that false prophets would "wear a rough garment to deceive" (Zechariah 13:4). Yahushua warned of prophets "in sheep's clothing" (Matthew 7:15) and of scribes who "love to go in long clothing" and to be greeted in the marketplaces (Mark 12:38). The robe, the shaved head, and the bowl are the very marks Scripture says not to trust.</p>
    <p>The gift turned too. When Saul went to Samuel, his servant brought a small gift as a courtesy (1 Samuel 9:7-8). It was a gift, not a deposit. Nothing in Scripture makes the man of God a field where giving earns merit.</p>
    <h2>The Same Turn in Christian Circles</h2>
    <p>Rome built its own order of robed holy men: monks and nuns under vows, and a priesthood marked by collar and vestment. Many Protestants still give the man in the pulpit a weight his words have not earned. The Bereans did the opposite. They received Paul's word gladly and then "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11).</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Scripture never told Israel to look for the robe. It told them to test the word. A man is a man of God when Yahuah's words are in his mouth and they lead back to Yahuah's commandments.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Buddhism blesses the man who sees the monk; Scripture blesses the man who tests the messenger by the Word (Deuteronomy 13:1-4; Acts 17:11).</p>
    <p>Revelation gives the mark of the true messenger: "the testimony of Yahushua is the spirit of prophecy" (Revelation 19:10). The testimony he carries, not the cloth he wears.</p>
